WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
You can now upload any video and explore it like a 3D world you can walk through in real time. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 173. Here's something that's going to change how you look at a clip on your phone. A developer posted a tiny web tool on X that turns ordinary videos into interactive 3.5D experiences. 3.5D just means you get depth, plus a slice of time you can push around. You also control how

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:31.720
how see through each moment looks,

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:34.400
so the clip stops feeling like a flat screen.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:36.840
You choose how many still pictures

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.900
from the clip sit in front of you at once.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.280
Those still pictures are called frames,

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.540
and a frame is one snapshot inside a video.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.480
Think of frames like pages in a flip book.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:49.260
One page is a pose.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.280
Many pages make motion.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:53.580
You can cut across those pages,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.080
peek at later pages, or fade them

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.380
so the view stays readable.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:01.800
picture, Google Street View, but you are strolling through seconds of a clip

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:07.100
instead of down a block. The knobs feel like the sliders in a video game. You

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:12.300
nudge one and the whole scene shifts. Volume resolution is how packed the view

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:17.760
feels. Crank it up and the sculpture gets denser. Time depth is how far you may

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:23.000
peek ahead or back, like leaning around a corner in time. Sinked playback keeps

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.960
keeps every layer moving together, so the motion does not fall out of step.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.380
Pre-frame opacity fades what already happened.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:33.820
Post-frame opacity fades what has not happened yet.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:39.640
That fade is why the scene can stay clear instead of turning into a smudge of overlapping pictures.

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:45.580
Time-slice surfaces let you cut the clip at one instant and inspect that cut as a flat

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:45.940
layer.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:51.360
You can tip that layer and study it from any angle, the way you might turn a glass figurine

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:51.980
in your hand.

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:54.820
The whole tool was built in one evening.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.220
The creator saw a similar idea and decided to tinker.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:04.080
The tinkering happened with Astra, an AI coding helper, meaning software that helps you

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:05.440
write programs by chatting.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.420
Passive watching becomes active exploring.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.740
You are not just pressing play, you are walking the clip.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:17.620
That could help a student studying a sports move, an artist remixing footage, or anyone

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:19.000
hunting a new joke angle.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:25.760
It also shows a bigger shift. Strong AI ideas are landing as free microtools in a browser,

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:31.840
not only as giant company launches. If you have a short video on your phone, open

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:37.680
the original post on X, upload the clip, and try the sliders. Notice how the scene turns

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:43.540
into a tiny sculpture made of moving pictures you can hold and tilt. The creator even asked

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:48.980
what to add next, so a note from you could shape the following version. It all runs in

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:55.200
browser, no special software, no expensive machine. A 14-year-old can start the same day.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:00.920
The computer is not painting extra movie magic on top of your clip. You already know the

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:06.520
feeling of a stack of photos of one scene, each taken a second apart. Your brain fills

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:18.960
the gaps. This tool lines up every frame of the video the same way, all at once. Imagine

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.060
Sliding sideways is walking through time.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:25.040
Moving forward is stepping through space inside that sandwich.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:30.500
You can freeze the whole stack, tilt it, or peel a layer to see what just happened or

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:31.340
what comes next.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:35.840
Simple math decides which layers to show or hide when you move a slider.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:37.660
Nothing new is invented.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:43.680
That is why a change in time depth or a fade on earlier frames snaps the view into a new

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:44.600
shape instantly.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.620
Volume resolution is sandwich thickness.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:53.040
more layers make a dense, detailed bite. Fewer layers stay light and readable.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:58.040
Synced playback is the toothpick holding the sandwich together, so every layer

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:02.580
stays in time while you rotate it. After you move a control, the computer

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:07.560
recalculates which frames belong on screen at that exact moment. You get the

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:12.420
feeling of flying the original clip without adding extra video files. Once a

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:16.680
video is just a stack of pictures you can peel, those sliders start to make

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:23.000
every day's sense. Chat GPT can now generate custom sticker images you can save and send in

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:28.940
messaging apps and gadget reports. You type a short description and it returns a small picture

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:34.080
with a see through background like a personal emoji. You can make stickers of your drawings,

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:41.380
your pet or an inside joke with zero design training. Anyone with a free chat GPT account can do this.

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:46.000
Open the chat GPT website, start a new chat, and type a request.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:47.820
Try this exact line.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:53.500
Create a sticker of a sleepy cat wearing sunglasses, transparent background, cute style.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:58.540
Download the image, then add it to your sticker pack in the Messages app or WhatsApp.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:02.480
Keep tweaking the wording until it matches the joke in your head.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.160
One sticker often takes less than a minute.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:07.900
You could build a whole set in a study break.

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:12.980
Because the background is see-through, the sticker sits in a chat the way official packs do.

